@node Diagnostics
@chapter Diagnostics

libdtfl carries a port of the timely diagnostics tools: a live console
that reconstructs the operator graph, scheduling and arrangement sizes
of a running computation.  It is built on the structured event logging
every worker produces.

@section Structured event logging

A worker emits structured events on named streams: @code{"timely"} (the
dataflow structure and scheduling), @code{"differential/arrange"}
(arrangement batch, merge and trace events) and @code{"communication"}
(message send and receive, thread state).  This is distinct from the
human-readable diagnostic sink of @code{dtfl_set_log_hook}
(@pxref{Error handling}); the structured log carries machine-readable
events.

A program subscribes to a stream and receives each batch of events
already decoded, so a C caller never touches the wire bytes.  Register
before the workers start with @code{dtfl_config_log_subscribe}, so every
worker installs the subscriber as it comes up, or on a single worker you
own with @code{dtfl_worker_log_subscribe}.  The subscriber is called on
the worker thread with a @code{dtfl_log_batch}; the accessors
(@code{dtfl_log_batch_len}, @code{dtfl_log_event_kind_at} and the
per-event field readers) read it.  The batch and every pointer it hands
out are valid only for the duration of the call.

@section The in-process console

The diagnostics session subscribes to a worker's @code{"timely"} and
@code{"differential/arrange"} streams, keeps the live set of operators,
channels and statistics, and serves them to browser clients over a
WebSocket as a stream of JSON frames.  The upstream console renders the
frames unmodified.

@example
dtfl_diag_session *s = dtfl_diag_session_new(worker);
dtfl_diag_server *srv = NULL;
if (dtfl_worker_index(worker) == 0)
        dtfl_diag_server_start(s, "127.0.0.1", 51371, &srv);
/* build and run the user dataflow; step the worker as usual */
dtfl_diag_server_stop(srv);
dtfl_diag_session_free(s);
@end example

The session is worker-owned: create it, pump it and free it on the
worker thread.  The server runs its accept and send loop on a background
thread and talks to the session through an internal queue, so no session
handle crosses the thread boundary.

By default the server binds @code{127.0.0.1}, so operator names and
timings are not published to the network without authentication; this is
a deliberate divergence from the upstream default of @code{0.0.0.0}
(@pxref{Porting notes}).  Pass a different interface to
@code{dtfl_diag_server_start} to restore the upstream behavior.

@section The command-line tool

@command{dtfl-diag} is the out-of-process counterpart, the C port of
timely's @command{tdiag}.  It reconstructs the operator graph
(@command{graph}), the timing profile (@command{profile}) or the
arrangement sizes (@command{arrangements}) of a computation, from log
streams the source program ships to it over TCP, or from a recorded
stream read with @option{-f}.  A source program connects its loggers to
the address @command{dtfl-diag} listens on through
@code{dtfl_config_from_env}, which reads @env{TIMELY_WORKER_LOG_ADDR}
and @env{DIFFERENTIAL_LOG_ADDR}.  See @code{dtfl-diag(1)} for the full
set of options.

@section Static probes

A library built with USDT probes (@option{--enable-usdt},
@pxref{Installation}) carries static probes under the provider
@code{dtfl}, which DTrace, SystemTap, @command{bpftrace} and
@command{perf} can attach to with no change to the program.  An untraced probe is a single @code{nop}; the probes use no
semaphores.  The probes fire once per operator schedule, park, unpark,
batch and merge, never in a per-record loop.  Every integer argument is
a @code{uint64_t}; addresses are @code{const void *} and are useful only
to match one probe's firing with another's.

@c usdt-probes-begin: tests/dist/test_usdt.sh reads the @item lines.
@table @asis
@item @code{dtfl:operator_start} (5 arguments)
@itemx @code{dtfl:operator_end} (5 arguments)
A scope is about to run one of its operators, and has just run it.
Arguments: @code{worker} (the worker index), @code{dataflow} (the
dataflow index), @code{id} (the operator's worker-unique identifier, as
in the @code{"timely"} log), @code{index} (its index within its
scope), then for @code{operator_start} the operator's name
(@code{const char *}) and for @code{operator_end} @code{incomplete}
(1 when the operator asked to be scheduled again, otherwise 0).  A nested
scope is itself an operator of its parent, so its start and end
bracket those of its children.

@item @code{dtfl:park} (3 arguments)
@itemx @code{dtfl:park_end} (2 arguments)
A worker with nothing to do is about to park, and has returned from
parking.  @code{park} has @code{worker}, @code{timeout_ns} (the longest
the worker will sleep; @code{UINT64_MAX} means until woken) and
@code{parker} (@code{const void *}); @code{park_end} has @code{worker}
and @code{parker}.  The worker returns at once when it already has a
wakeup pending.

@item @code{dtfl:unpark} (1 argument)
Some thread is waking a worker: @code{parker} (@code{const void *}), the
same address as in that worker's @code{park}.  It fires on every wakeup,
whether or not the worker is parked.

@item @code{dtfl:batch_seal} (3 arguments)
A newly built batch enters a trace (an arrangement's, a reduce
output's or an upsert's).  Arguments: @code{trace}
(@code{const void *}, the same in that trace's merge probes),
@code{batch} (@code{const void *}) and @code{updates}, the batch's
update count, which is 0 for an empty batch that only advances the
trace's frontier.

@item @code{dtfl:merge_begin} (4 arguments)
@itemx @code{dtfl:merge_end} (5 arguments)
A trace starts merging two batches at one level of its spine, and
finishes.  Arguments: @code{trace} (@code{const void *}), @code{level}
(the spine level; a batch at level @var{k} counts as 2@sup{@var{k}}
updates for merge accounting), @code{len1} and @code{len2} (the input
batches' update counts), and for @code{merge_end} @code{updates}, the
merged batch's update count after compaction.  A merge proceeds in
fueled steps across many operator schedules between the two.
@end table
@c usdt-probes-end

@command{bpftrace} counts operator schedules by name, for every process
using the library (the path is where @file{libdtfl.so} is installed):

@example
sudo bpftrace -e 'usdt:/usr/local/lib/libdtfl.so:dtfl:operator_start
    @{ @@[str(arg4)] = count(); @}'
@end example

@noindent
and shows how long workers sleep when they park:

@example
sudo bpftrace -e '
usdt:/usr/local/lib/libdtfl.so:dtfl:park @{ @@t[tid] = nsecs; @}
usdt:/usr/local/lib/libdtfl.so:dtfl:park_end /@@t[tid]/ @{
    @@us = hist((nsecs - @@t[tid]) / 1000); delete(@@t[tid]); @}'
@end example

@command{readelf -n libdtfl.so} lists the probes the library carries.
The library is also built with frame pointers by default, so
@command{perf} and @command{bpftrace} can take stack traces without
DWARF unwinding.
